/**
 * @mainpage TIVIDY - generic workflow management
 *
 * TIVIDY models the structure, state and progression of a workflow, not the business logic of
 * its steps. The same building blocks serve any industry; the two concrete scenarios are an
 * Incident Response workflow (Power Outage) and a Job Application workflow (Tech Company).
 * Component ids are templated (`int` or `std::string`); there are no other template parameters.
 *
 * @section overview How the pieces fit together
 * 1. A **Builder** (driven by the WorkflowDirector for the two scenarios, or by the user for a
 *    custom workflow) assembles a WorkflowDefinition: a **Composite** tree of WorkGroup and
 *    WorkItem objects, wrapped in **Decorators** (AuditedWork, EscalatedWork, ApprovedWork).
 * 2. The definition is a **Prototype**: clone() it to get a running instance. Only an untouched
 *    definition can be cloned.
 * 3. Participants are added to the running workflow's pool. They are owned by the application.
 * 4. start() releases the first step. All work is done by **Commands** (assign, start, complete,
 *    reject, cancel, escalate, approve) executed through the workflow's command history.
 *    Each command takes **Mementos** first, so the newest command can be undone when nothing
 *    after it has moved on.
 * 5. Every WorkItem is the context of a **State** machine. When an item completes it notifies
 *    the **Mediator**, which releases the next step once the current step (an item, or a whole
 *    group of parallel children) is complete.
 * 6. Assignment uses **Strategy** objects that filter the workflow's whole participant pool;
 *    without strategies the participants are listed explicitly. Escalation and approval walk a
 *    **Chain of Responsibility**, one handler per Escalate or Approve command.
 * 7. **Visitors** (TreePrinter, ProgressReport) and an **Iterator** (DepthFirstIterator) read
 *    the tree. The TividyFacade is an optional single entry point to all of this.
 *
 * @section patterns Design pattern map
 * | Pattern | Classes |
 * |---|---|
 * | Prototype | WorkflowPrototype, WorkflowDefinition |
 * | Builder | Builder, WorkflowBuilder, WorkflowDirector (product: WorkflowDefinition) |
 * | Composite | WorkComponent, WorkGroup, WorkItem |
 * | Decorator | WorkItemDecorator, AuditedWork, EscalatedWork, ApprovedWork |
 * | State | WorkItemState and its concrete states (context: WorkItem) |
 * | Strategy | AssignmentStrategy, RoleAssignment, LeastLoadedAssignment, RuleAssignment |
 * | Chain of Responsibility | Handler, LevelHandler, FallbackHandler |
 * | Mediator | Mediator, WorkflowMediator |
 * | Command | Command, WorkCommand and the concrete commands, CommandHistory (invoker) |
 * | Memento | WorkMemento (originators: WorkItem, EscalatedWork, ApprovedWork; caretaker: WorkCommand) |
 * | Iterator | WorkIterator, DepthFirstIterator |
 * | Visitor | WorkVisitor, TreePrinter, ProgressReport |
 * | Template Method | WorkCommand::execute/undo, Handler::handle/copy |
 * | Facade | TividyFacade |
 *
 * @section lifecycle Work item lifecycle
 * | State | Allowed operations |
 * |---|---|
 * | Created | release (Available), cancel |
 * | Available | assign (Assigned), cancel |
 * | Assigned | start (InProgress), cancel |
 * | InProgress | complete, reject, escalate (Escalated), approve (Approval), cancel |
 * | Escalated | complete, reject, cancel, escalate (next handler), approve (hand-over) |
 * | Approval | complete, reject, cancel, approve (next approver) |
 *
 * Completed, Rejected and Cancelled are end states. Rejected, Cancelled and unfinished steps
 * halt the workflow because the Mediator releases the next step only after a completion. An item
 * rejected from Approval is the one exception: the Start command moves it back to InProgress.
 *
 * @section escalation Escalation and approval
 * - **Escalate**: the first command moves InProgress to Escalated and passes the request to the
 *   first handler; each further command hands it to the next handler; when the next handler is
 *   the FallbackHandler the item completes immediately. A Complete command (a handler resolved
 *   it) also ends the escalation.
 * - **Approve**: the first command moves InProgress to Approval and passes the request to the
 *   first approver; each further command means the previous approver signed off; reaching the
 *   last approver completes the item.
 * - An item with both decorators flows **Escalate, then Approve, then Completed**: resolving the
 *   escalation hands the item straight to the approval chain.
 *
 * @section usage Example
 * @code
 * TividyFacade<int> facade;
 * WorkflowDefinition<int>* prototype = facade.constructIncidentResponseWorkflow({1, 2, 3, 4, 5});
 * WorkflowDefinition<int>* run = facade.cloneWorkflow(prototype);
 * run->addParticipant(&dana);
 * facade.startWorkflow(run);
 * RoleAssignment<int> role("dispatcher");
 * facade.assignWithStrategies(run, 1, std::vector<AssignmentStrategy<int>*>(1, &role), 1);
 * facade.startWork(run, 1);
 * facade.completeWork(run, 1);
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section build Building and running
 * `make` builds the `tividy` demo, `make run` runs both scenarios, `make test` runs the
 * regression test with AddressSanitizer, `make docs` generates this documentation.
 */
